    {
      "parameters": {
        "jsCode": "const f = $('Form Trigger1').first().json;\nconst topic = String(f['Topic'] || '').trim();\nconst learned = String(f['What I learned'] || '').trim();\nif (!topic || !learned) throw new Error('Please fill Topic and What I learned in the form');\nreturn [{\n  json: {\n    topic,\n    audience: f['Who is this for?'] || 'Students and beginners',\n    angle: learned\n  }\n}];"
      },
      "id": "d963a488-244f-4cc9-a4bd-71b2a283e792",
      "name": "Duplicate Check",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.code",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [
        384,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=Topic: {{ $json.topic }}\nTarget audience: {{ $json.audience }}\n\nWhat I actually learned (my own notes, use these as the facts):\n{{ $json.angle }}\n\nWrite the LinkedIn post.",
        "options": {
          "systemMessage": "=You are ghostwriting a LinkedIn post that must sound exactly like this person, written in first person (\"I\").\n\nABOUT THEM (from their own ChatGPT memory; treat as the source of truth):\n{{ $('Config').first().json.myProfile }}\n\nTHEIR GOALS AND INTERESTS:\n{{ $('Config').first().json.myGoals }}\n\nVoice: write like I'm texting a smart friend about something I just realised or learned. Simple everyday words, short sentences, contractions (I'm, don't, it's). Casual, honest, a little curious. Copy my tone and opinions from the profile; if it describes my writing style, follow that first.\n\nSound human, not like AI or a corporate page:\n- Never use phrases like: delve, unlock, game-changer, leverage, in today's fast-paced world, let's dive in, here's the thing, it's not just X, it's Y.\n- No em dashes, no hype, no buzzword lists, no motivational-poster lines.\n- Don't force a rigid template. Start with a natural opening line (something I'd actually say), keep a conversational flow, and let paragraph lengths vary.\n- End with one genuine question, like I'm really asking my friends what they think.\n- 90-160 words. Exactly 3 relevant hashtags at the very end. Plain text only: no markdown, no bold, no emojis unless my profile says I use them.\n\nTruthfulness rules:\n- Only mention experiences, projects, skills and results that appear in the profile above or in my notes under \"What I actually learned\". Never invent personal stories, achievements, numbers, clients or news.\n- Explain only what my notes say, at a beginner level. Do not add extra technical claims, tools or steps that are not in my notes. Write it as something I learned, not as expert advice.\n- If no real experience fits, share it as something I'm learning, an honest opinion, or a question from my current stage.\n- Never reveal private details (health, family, finances, exact location, contact info) even if they appear in the profile.\n\nReturn only the post text."
        }
      },
